![HackerNews](cover.png)

In the following article we will create a small _Hacker News_ client using Phoenix Framework and Surface UI (LiveView), applying the concepts of _MVVM_ (Model - View - View Model) for the architecture design.

The concepts are based on the articles by [Matteo Manferdini](https://matteomanferdini.com/mvvm-swiftui/) who focuses on the mobile technology _SwiftUI_, which is very similar to declarative technologies like _LiveView_.

The _MVVM_ pattern incorporates good ideas and some difficulties due to its different interpretations. In this article we will see its advantages and how to navigate its challenges.

## Requirements

To be able to follow this article we recommend having _Elixir_ installed and a project configured with _SurfaceUI_.

- <https://github.com/ElixirCL/ElixirCL/wiki/%5BTips%5D-Instalar-Surface-UI>
- <https://github.com/ElixirCL/ElixirCL/wiki/%5BTips%5D-Ambiente-de-Desarrollo-Elixir-en-Windows>

## Hacker News Client

To have an example of _MVVM_ in _LiveView_, we are going to create a small application for _Hacker News_, a news portal for _devs_. We will use its _API_ to get ten stories from the best stories section.

You can see the result here

```github
ElixirCL/surface-hackernews | Un pequeño cliente de Hacker News construido con Phoenix Framework y Surface UI
```

- <https://github.com/HackerNews/API>
- <https://news.ycombinator.com/best>

## MVVM and MVC Patterns?

Current technology allows us to create complex applications with relative ease, which has led some people to use practices that hinder the maintainability and robustness of software solutions.

To achieve software products that are robust and easy to maintain over time, more is needed than putting together pieces of code scattered without a cohesive order. While one can search on _Google_ to solve specific tasks, copying and pasting code so that it works in some way. When moving beyond the basics and into the professional territory, we will inevitably encounter difficulties.

For this reason the industry has developed patterns such as _MVC_ and _MVVM_.

### What is the MVC pattern?

The _Model-View-Controller_ (_MVC_) pattern is one of the first you should learn. It is so fundamental that it has survived decades in the industry and its ideas have spread across many platforms. It is the father of many other derived patterns such as _MVVM_, among others.

![MVC](mvc.png)

This pattern is essential because it helps answer one of the most common questions:

> Where should I put this piece of code?

The _MVC_ pattern is an architecture pattern. It gives you a map of the application structure and, as its name says, it consists of three layers.

- The `model` layer (_model_): It is the layer that handles the data and business logic, independent of its visual representation.

- The `view` layer (_view_): It is the layer that shows the information to the user and allows interactions, independent of the data layer.

- The `controller` layer (_controller_): It is the layer that acts as a bridge between model and view. It stores and manipulates the application state and provides data to the view, interpreting user actions according to the business rules.

The following diagram from Apple shows a bit of the relationship between views and controllers.

![Apple MVC](apple.avif)

- <https://developer.apple.com/library/archive/featuredarticles/ViewControllerPGforiPhoneOS/index.html>

The main problem with _MVC_ and the reason why other derived patterns were born is the tendency of controllers to grow exponentially. Even to the point of being called _Massive View Controllers_, due to the number of responsibilities they had to fulfill.

### What is the MVVM pattern?

The _Model-View-ViewModel_ (_MVVM_) pattern is an architecture pattern that makes it easy to structure the application by dividing it into three roles.

![MVVM](mvvm.png)

- The `model` (_model_): represents the application's data and business logic.

- The `view` (_view_): Shows the information to the user and allows interaction.

- The `view-model` (_view-model_): Acts as a bridge between the view and model layers. It contains the view state and handles the interaction logic.

### Differences between MVC and MVVM?

When comparing the _MVC_ and _MVVM_ patterns the similarity is notable and they are almost identical.

The main difference is that _MVC_ emphasizes `controllers`. Responsible for handling interactions for several views. In contrast, in _MVVM_ the `view-model` is a single component that controls the behavior and state of a single view. Commonly represented as a component.

Another difference is the way of communication between the view and its controller. In _MVC_ the view and the controller have defined functions that are called imperatively to report an action or to request updating the information in the view. On the other hand, in _MVVM_ the view and the view-model are joined by a binding mechanism (`binding`) that automatically reports interactions performed in the view and changes occurring in the view-model. These binding mechanisms vary depending on the platform; in the case of _LiveView_ everything comes configured out of the box and it is simpler and more intuitive.

### The importance of MVVM

Using an architecture pattern like _MVVM_ with clearly defined roles helps us fulfill design principles such as separation of concerns. Which is a cornerstone for keeping code well organized, easily understandable, and whose unit tests are viable to implement.

Using architecture patterns like _MVVM_ is extremely important. Even though _LiveView_ gives us innovative tools to build our applications, if we do not use architecture patterns the code will accumulate, increasing in complexity, finally creating massive monoliths that are difficult to maintain and test.

The fact that _LiveView_ automatically handles view updates does not justify abandoning the good software development practices that have existed for decades across multiple platforms.

- <https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Separation_of_concerns>
- <https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coupling_(computer_programming)>

### Is MVVM MVC?

The layers of _MVC_ interact and are interpreted depending on some factors such as:

- The platform where it is implemented.
- The professional's experience and their interpretation of the pattern.
- The fashion of the day (Devs can still follow fashions).

The _Model-View-ViewModel_ (_MVVM_) pattern is mainly a version of _MVC_ under a different name.

Although there are slight differences, the concepts of _MVC_ and _MVVM_ can perfectly well be used in a unified way without problems. To simplify, we only refer to it as _MVVM_, since it is one of the valid ways to interpret this pattern.

### Why is MVVM ideal for LiveView?

Let's go over the different _LiveView_ tools and how we can extrapolate them to the concepts of _MVVM_.

``` =html
<table>
  <thead>
    <tr><th>Phoenix LiveView</th><th>MVVM</th><th>Description</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>LiveView</td><td>Controller</td><td>It is the main one in charge of managing general events and states or those related to the server and having a tree of views and view-models</td></tr>
    <tr><td>LiveComponent</td><td>View-Model</td><td>It is in charge of managing events and states related to the view and coordinating the fetching of data from the internet/database</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Component</td><td>View</td><td>It is the view and only has properties to display the data delivered by the view-model</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
```

Phoenix does not force you to follow an explicit architectural pattern. However _LiveView_ is particularly appropriate for the _MVVM_ pattern. It offers components that are independent of the data that integrate very well into the `view` layer of the _MVVM_ pattern. In addition _LiveView_ provides mechanisms to bind views to data and automatically update user interfaces when the associated data has changes.

The following diagram shows a possible architecture organization following _MVVM_ with _LiveView_.

![LiveView MVVM](liveview.avif)

### Beyond MVVM

Architecture patterns like _MVC_ and _MVVM_ have their focus on applications where we mainly have user interactions (_UX_), but often applications have to communicate with external services and other elements that need other ways to manage our code architecture.

For this we recommend using patterns like those defined in Domain Driven Design and Hexagonal architecture.

