## Hacker News API Project

The project will consist of the following files

```txt
├── lib
│   ├── hackernews
│   │   ├── infra
│   │   │   └── hackernews
│   │   │       └── beststories
│   │   │           ├── api.ex
│   │   │           └── mock.ex
│   │   ├── models
│   │   │   └── hackernews
│   │   │       └── beststories
│   │   │           ├── model.ex
│   │   │           ├── queries.ex
│   │   │           └── types.ex
│   ├── hackernews_web
│   │   ├── live
│   │   │   └── hackernews
│   │   │       └── beststories
│   │   │           ├── components
│   │   │           │   └── entry.ex
│   │   │           ├── controller.ex
│   │   │           └── viewmodel.ex
│   │   ├── router.ex
├── test
│   ├── hackernews_web
│   │   └── live
│   │       └── hackernews
│   │           └── beststories
│   │               └── beststories_test.exs
└── └
```

### infra

The infrastructure is all those services external to our application. Here we will find the elements that interact with them. This would be considered a "_Boundary_" layer.

#### api.ex and mock.ex

Part of the infrastructure, it contains the calls to the _HackerNews_ _API_. It does not perform any kind of parameter validation or data transformation, since that is the responsibility of other elements. It simply focuses on calling the external server and returning the result.

Note that the `base_url` is modified in the `test` environment to use a _Mock API_ that we use for validation in the tests.

This is part of a _mock_ technique that allows us to simplify the tests without coupling our client.

You can read about this technique here.

<https://github.com/ElixirCL/ElixirCL/wiki/%5BArticulo%5D-Crear-Mocks-de-Endpoints-en-Phoenix>

```elixir
defmodule HackerNews.Infra.HackerNews.BestStories.API do
  @base_url if Mix.env() == :test,
              do: "http://localhost:4002/mocks/hackernews",
              else: "https://hacker-news.firebaseio.com/v0/"

  def all() do
    Req.new(
      base_url: @base_url,
      url: "beststories.json"
    )
    |> Req.get()
  end

  def get(story: id) do
    Req.new(
      base_url: @base_url,
      url: "item/#{id}.json"
    )
    |> Req.get()
  end
end
```

### models

The files in this context are in charge of processing the calls and responses to the infrastructure components.

#### types.ex

The structures that will be used for calls and responses. Its only responsibility is to standardize the data, validate it and transform it into structures.

In this context only the _Item_ structure was considered, which processes the _HackerNews_ response and will be used later in the view.

```elixir
defmodule HackerNews.Models.HackerNews.BestStories.Types.Item do
  defstruct ~w(id comment_count score author title date url footnote)a

  defp get_footnote(json) do
    url =
      Access.get(json, "url", "")
      |> URI.parse()

    time =
      Access.get(json, "time", System.os_time())
      |> DateTime.from_unix!()

    %{host: url.host, time: time, by: Access.get(json, "by", "unknown")}
  end

  def decode(json) do
    %__MODULE__{
      id: get_in(json, ["id"]),
      comment_count: get_in(json, ["descendants"]),
      score: get_in(json, ["score"]),
      author: get_in(json, ["by"]),
      title: get_in(json, ["title"]),
      date: get_in(json, ["time"]),
      url: get_in(json, ["url"]),
      footnote: get_footnote(json)
    }
  end
end
```

#### queries.ex

It is in charge of making the different calls to the API using the endpoints with _GET_. This file is part of _CQRS_ (Command, Query, Responsibility, Segregation). A pattern that recommends separating queries from operations. In the case of _HackerNews_ we only make queries, but if we wanted to perform operations we would have to have a `commands.ex` file for the _POST, PUT, PATCH and DELETE_ type API calls.

```elixir
defmodule HackerNews.Models.HackerNews.BestStories.Queries do
  alias HackerNews.Infra.HackerNews.BestStories.API

  require Logger

  def get_top_story_ids(amount \\ 10) do
    with {:ok, ids} <- API.all() do
      ids.body
      |> Enum.take(amount)
    else
      err ->
        Logger.error(err)
        []
    end
  end

  def get_story(id) do
    API.get(story: id)
  end

  def get_stories(ids) do
    ids
    |> Enum.map(&get_story(&1))
  end
end
```

#### model.ex

It is in charge of coordinating _Queries_ and _Types_, a kind of _facade_. We transform the responses from _Queries_ into structures defined in _Types_.

```elixir
defmodule HackerNews.Models.HackerNews.BestStories do
  alias __MODULE__.Types
  alias __MODULE__.Queries

  require Logger

  def top(amount \\ 10) do
    Queries.get_top_story_ids(amount)
    |> Queries.get_stories()
    |> Enum.map(fn
      {:error, error} ->
        Logger.error(error)
        nil

      {:ok, response} ->
        Types.Item.decode(response.body)
    end)
    |> Enum.filter(&(&1 != nil))
  end
end
```

### live

Directory that contains all the user interface (_UX_) files. Here we will use the _MVVM_ pattern to organize our files.

#### controller.ex

The one in charge of instantiating the _ViewModel_ and handling general or server events and managing properties such as parameters and session.

```elixir
defmodule HackerNewsWeb.HackerNews.Live.BestStories do
  use HackerNewsWeb, :surface_live_view

  alias __MODULE__.ViewModel

  def mount(_params, _session, socket) do
    {:ok, socket}
  end

  def render(assigns) do
    ~F"""
    <ViewModel id="beststories" />
    """
  end
end
```

#### viewmodel.ex

It is in charge of handling the view events and calling our models to obtain information.

Also note how a function is used to format the data before the view obtains and displays it.

```elixir
defmodule HackerNewsWeb.HackerNews.Live.BestStories.ViewModel do
  use Surface.LiveComponent

  alias HackerNewsWeb.HackerNews.Live.BestStories.View.Components.Entry
  alias HackerNews.Models.HackerNews.BestStories

  data entries, :list, default: []

  @impl true
  def mount(socket) do
    socket =
      socket
      |> assign(:entries, BestStories.top())

    {:ok, socket}
  end

  # This function is a small helper to have relative time.
  # To avoid using a library like Timex.
  # Extracted from: https://stackoverflow.com/a/65915005
  # And https://gist.github.com/h00s/b863579ec9c7b8c65311e6862298b7a0
  defp from_now_ago_in_words(later, now \\ DateTime.utc_now()) do

    seconds = DateTime.diff(now, later)
    minutes = round(seconds/60)

    case minutes do
      minutes when minutes in 0..1 ->
        case seconds do
          seconds when seconds in 0..4 ->
            "less than 5 seconds"
          seconds when seconds in 5..9 ->
            "less than 10 seconds"
          seconds when seconds in 10..19 ->
            "less than 20 seconds"
          seconds when seconds in 20..39 ->
            "half a minute"
          seconds when seconds in 40..59 ->
            "less than 1 minute"
          _ ->
            "1 minute"
        end
      minutes when minutes in 2..44 ->
        "#{minutes} minutes"
      minutes when minutes in 45..89 ->
        "about 1 hour"
      minutes when minutes in 90..1439 ->
        "about #{round(minutes/60)} hours"
      minutes when minutes in 1440..2519 ->
        "1 day"
      minutes when minutes in 2520..43199 ->
        "#{round(minutes/1440)} days"
      minutes when minutes in 43200..86399 ->
        "about 1 month"
      minutes when minutes in 86400..525599 ->
        "#{round(minutes/43200)} months"
      minutes when minutes in 525600..1051199 ->
        "1 year"
      _ ->
        "#{round(minutes/525600)} years"
    end
  end

  def render(assigns) do
    ~F"""
    <div id="beststories">
      <h1 class="text-5xl font-extrabold dark:text-white mb-10">HackerNews Best Stories</h1>
      {#for entry <- @entries}
        <Entry
          url={entry.url}
          title={entry.title}
          footnote={"#{entry.footnote.host} - #{from_now_ago_in_words(entry.footnote.time)} ago by #{entry.footnote.by}"}
          score={entry.score}
          comment_count={entry.comment_count}
        />
      {/for}
    </div>
    """
  end
end
```

#### components/entry.ex

The view is mainly created using components. In this case a single component that displays the data of a news story.

```elixir
defmodule HackerNewsWeb.HackerNews.Live.BestStories.View.Components.Entry do
  use Surface.Component

  prop url, :string
  prop title, :string
  prop footnote, :string
  prop score, :integer
  prop comment_count, :integer

  def render(assigns) do
    ~F"""
    <div class="entry mt-4">
      <h2 class="entry-title text-xl font-bold dark:text-white"><a class="entry-url" href={@url}>{@title}</a></h2>
      <h3 class="entry-footnote mt-2 text-lg dark:text-white">{@footnote}</h3> <div class="entry-stats flex mt-2">
        <span class="mr-2">🔼</span> <p class="entry-score font-bold">{@score}</p> <span class="mr-2 ml-4">💬</span> <p class="entry-comment-count font-bold">{@comment_count}</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    """
  end
end
```

#### test/beststories_test.exs

Thanks to the mock technique for the _API_ our tests only focus on evaluating whether the rendering is correct and contains the necessary information.

```elixir
defmodule HackerNewsWeb.HackerNews.Live.BestStoriesTest do
  @moduledoc false
  use HackerNewsWeb.ConnCase, async: true
  use Surface.LiveViewTest
  import Phoenix.LiveViewTest

  alias HackerNews.Infra.Mocks.HackerNews.BestStories.API, as: Mock

  @route "/"

  describe "Best Stories" do
    test "that displays the 10 best stories", %{conn: conn} do
      {:ok, liveview, html} = live(conn, @route)

      # first check if we have the container element
      assert liveview
             |> element("#beststories")
             |> has_element?() == true

      # then we use Floki to parse the html
      {:ok, document} = Floki.parse_document(html)

      entries =
        Floki.find(document, ".entry")

      assert Enum.count(entries) == 10

      titles = Floki.find(document, ".entry-title")
      |> Enum.map(fn {_htag, _hattrs, [{_atag, _aattrs, [title]}]} -> title end)

      assert titles == Enum.map(Mock.data, fn {_k, v} -> v["title"] end)
    end
  end
end
```

## Conclusion

Using patterns like _MVVM_ allows us to simplify our code organization, improve the experience when creating tests and have some standardization in projects.

However they are not the only patterns we can use, since _Phoenix_ projects go far beyond user interfaces; we have at our disposal a whole unified frontend and backend ecosystem.

Our applications have to answer the following questions, according to the *CRC* pattern:

- *Create*: How is the data created/obtained?.
- *Reduce*: What transformations does it need and how should they be done?.
- *Consume*: How do I show the result or consume that data?.

Following these concepts we will be able to organize and improve our software solutions so that they are robust, efficient and easy to maintain over time.
